# Copy to .env.local (dev) or configure via your deploy pipeline's env injection for
# .env.production -- Vite picks up whichever applies automatically, no code changes needed.
#
# Used only by Diagnostics.tsx's "More detail" links (Swagger/Actuator) -- both live outside
# /api entirely on the backend, so they can't go through the dev-server's /api proxy the rest of
# this app relies on. Every other API call in this app uses relative /api paths and needs no
# configuration here; don't add more entries to this file just to "be consistent" unless
# something else genuinely can't use the proxy either.
#
# Defaults to http://localhost:8080 if unset (matches vite.config.ts's own proxy target).
VITE_BACKEND_ORIGIN=http://localhost:8080

# The backend's own absolute origin for the actual axios client (src/api/client.ts) -- REQUIRED
# for any real deployment where this app and the backend don't share an origin (see that file's
# own doc comment). Distinct from VITE_BACKEND_ORIGIN above, which only covers a couple of
# manually-built human-facing links, not the API client itself. Leave unset for local dev.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=

# Firebase Web SDK config -- same purpose and same values as frontend/.env.example's own copy of
# these six (this app's VerifyPhone.tsx/ResetPassword.tsx use Firebase Phone Authentication too;
# see src/lib/firebase.ts and src/lib/phoneAuth.ts). Get these from Firebase Console -> Project
# Settings -> General -> "Your apps" -> the SDK config snippet -- the same Firebase project as
# frontend/'s, and the same project the backend's GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS service account
# belongs to.
#
# That "same project" rule is per-tier, not global: for the deployed Dev tier
# (dev-admin.finoratech.info), these six belong to a Firebase project separate from production's,
# set in Cloudflare Pages' "Preview" bucket (not "Production") -- see deployment-guide.md's "Dev
# environment" section.
VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
VITE_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# Google Identity Services client id (Admin MFA UI, SEC-03) -- used ONLY as the re-authentication
# step before disabling MFA on a GOOGLE-signInMethod admin account (GoogleReauthPrompt); this app
# has no "Sign in with Google" login button. Same client id frontend/.env.example uses, but Google
# Cloud Console's "Authorized JavaScript origins" for that OAuth client must separately list this
# app's own origin(s) too -- adding a client id here does nothing until that's done. Leave unset to
# hide the re-auth button entirely for a GOOGLE-signInMethod admin (they'd have no way to disable
# MFA at all in that state; see GoogleReauthPrompt.tsx's own doc comment).
VITE_GOOGLE_LOGIN_CLIENT_ID=

# Sentry crash reporting. Leave empty to disable it entirely -- src/lib/monitoring.ts no-ops when
# this is unset, so local development and the test suite never emit events. A DSN is not a secret
# in the way an API key is (it only permits writing events, and ships inside every client bundle),
# but it is a real per-project value: get it from Sentry -> Project Settings -> Client Keys (DSN).
#
# What gets sent is deliberately far narrower than Sentry's defaults, because this app's URLs carry
# password-reset tokens and the customer ids an admin was viewing --
# see src/lib/monitoring.ts for exactly what is stripped and why.
#
# BUILD-TIME, not runtime. Vite inlines this into the bundle when it builds, so it must be set as a
# build environment variable on Cloudflare Pages -- setting it after the fact does nothing. The
# upside of that inlining, measured: with this unset the whole Sentry SDK is tree-shaken out of the
# bundle (verified: zero occurrences of "sentry" in dist/), so crash reporting costs nothing at all
# until it is actually configured. With it set the bundle grows by ~30 kB gzipped.
VITE_SENTRY_DSN=
